/* Exam paper A: 100 questions. t = topic key (see TOPICS in app.js).
   SBA: o = five options, a = index of the answer. SAQ: acc = accepted keyword groups (each "alt|alt"; all groups must match),
   no = phrases that make an answer wrong, m = model answer. Explanations avoid option letters because options can be shuffled. */
window.EXAM_PAPERS=window.EXAM_PAPERS||{};
EXAM_PAPERS.A={title:'Paper A',blurb:'Mixed clinical paper: haemostasis, healing, innate and adaptive immunity, microbes and drugs.',qs:[
/* ---------- haemostasis ---------- */
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 24-year-old woman has had heavy periods since menarche, frequent nosebleeds and prolonged oozing after a wisdom tooth extraction. Her mother has similar problems. Platelets 260 ×10⁹/L, PT 12 s (normal), APTT 39 s (reference 25–35). What is the most likely diagnosis?',
 o:['Haemophilia A','Von Willebrand disease','Immune thrombocytopenia','Factor V Leiden','Vitamin K deficiency'],a:1,
 e:'Mucosal bleeding (menorrhagia, epistaxis, after dental work) points to a primary haemostasis problem, and autosomal dominant inheritance affecting women fits von Willebrand disease, the commonest inherited bleeding disorder. The APTT is slightly long because vWF carries and protects factor VIII. Haemophilia A is X-linked (affected women are rare) and gives deep joint and muscle bleeds. ITP would lower the platelet count. Factor V Leiden causes clots, not bleeding. Vitamin K deficiency prolongs the PT first.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 6-year-old boy has recurrent nosebleeds and easy bruising. The blood film shows giant platelets and the count is 85 ×10⁹/L. Platelets aggregate normally with ADP and collagen but not with ristocetin, and adding normal plasma does not correct this. Which platelet component is missing?',
 o:['Glycoprotein Ib–IX–V','Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa','Von Willebrand factor','Cyclo-oxygenase-1','P2Y₁₂ receptor'],a:0,
 e:'Ristocetin makes vWF bind platelet GPIb. Failure with ristocetin, giant platelets and mild thrombocytopenia is Bernard–Soulier syndrome (GPIb–IX–V deficiency). In von Willebrand disease the ristocetin defect corrects when normal plasma supplies vWF, and platelets are normal in size. Glanzmann thrombasthenia (GPIIb/IIIa) gives the opposite pattern: absent aggregation with ADP and collagen but a normal ristocetin response. COX-1 and P2Y₁₂ defects would impair ADP or collagen responses.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 61-year-old woman with a new DVT is started on warfarin alone, without heparin. On day 3 she develops painful, dark purple, sharply demarcated skin lesions on her thighs and breasts that become necrotic. Deficiency of which protein makes this complication more likely?',
 o:['Protein C','Antithrombin','Factor VIII','Plasminogen','Von Willebrand factor'],a:0,
 e:'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis. Protein C is vitamin K dependent with a short half-life, so it falls within a day, before prothrombin and factors IX and X. For the first few days the patient is relatively pro-thrombotic, causing microvascular thrombosis in fatty skin. People with inherited protein C deficiency are most at risk, which is why heparin is overlapped when warfarin is started for a clot. Antithrombin is not vitamin K dependent.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 66-year-old man is on IV unfractionated heparin after a hip replacement. On day 7 his platelet count has fallen from 290 to 95 ×10⁹/L and he develops a new DVT in the other leg. What is the most appropriate next step?',
 o:['Increase the heparin dose to treat the new clot','Stop heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban','Switch to low molecular weight heparin','Transfuse platelets','Give protamine and stop all anticoagulation'],a:1,
 e:'A fall in platelets of more than 50% 5–10 days after starting heparin, with a new thrombosis, is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. IgG antibodies against heparin–platelet factor 4 complexes activate platelets, so it is a prothrombotic state despite the low count. All heparin must stop (LMWH cross-reacts) and a non-heparin anticoagulant (argatroban, or a DOAC or fondaparinux per local policy) is needed. Platelet transfusion can fuel thrombosis; stopping all anticoagulation leaves the clots untreated.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 34-year-old woman has sudden pleuritic chest pain after a long-haul flight. Heart rate 88, oxygen saturation 98%, no signs of DVT; her two-level Wells score makes PE unlikely. D-dimer is negative. What is the correct interpretation?',
 o:['PE is very unlikely and no imaging for PE is needed','PE is confirmed and she needs anticoagulation','The test should be repeated in 24 hours before deciding','A negative D-dimer is unreliable because the test has low sensitivity','She should be anticoagulated and have a CT pulmonary angiogram'],a:0,
 e:'D-dimer is a fibrin breakdown product made when cross-linked fibrin is lysed. The test is highly sensitive but not specific, so a negative result with low clinical probability effectively rules out PE. A positive result would not confirm PE (it rises after surgery, in infection, pregnancy, cancer and old age). In a high-probability patient you would go straight to imaging rather than rely on D-dimer.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 58-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis has easy bruising. PT 22 s (normal about 12 s), APTT mildly prolonged, platelets 70 ×10⁹/L. After three days of IV vitamin K the PT is unchanged. What best explains this?',
 o:['Damaged hepatocytes cannot make clotting factors even with vitamin K available','Vitamin K was not absorbed from the gut','The enlarged spleen is destroying clotting factors','He has antibodies against vitamin K-dependent factors','Excess fibrinolysis is consuming prothrombin'],a:0,
 e:'The liver makes most clotting factors. In malabsorption or obstructive jaundice the PT corrects with IV vitamin K because the liver works but lacks the vitamin; in cirrhosis the synthetic machinery itself has failed. IV dosing bypasses absorption. The spleen sequesters platelets (hence the low count) but does not destroy clotting factors. Factor VII has the shortest half-life, so the PT is the most sensitive marker of liver synthetic function.'},
{t:'haem',type:'saq',q:'Warfarin stops the liver making active factors II, VII, IX and X. Name the enzyme it inhibits.',acc:['epoxide reductase|vkor'],m:'Vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1)',
 e:'Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, so vitamin K cannot be recycled to its reduced form. Without it, γ-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S) fails, and the factors cannot bind Ca²⁺ and phospholipid. Genetic variation in VKORC1 and CYP2C9 explains much of the variation in warfarin dose.'},
{t:'haem',type:'saq',q:'A 72-year-old with atrial fibrillation takes apixaban. Which activated clotting factor does apixaban directly inhibit?',acc:['xa|10a|factorx|factor10'],no:['xi','ixa','11','9a','xii'],m:'Factor Xa',
 e:'Apixaban, rivaroxaban and edoxaban (the "-xabans") are direct factor Xa inhibitors, blocking prothrombinase. Dabigatran is the direct thrombin (IIa) inhibitor. Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors; idarucizumab reverses dabigatran.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 31-year-old woman has a placental abruption and a large postpartum haemorrhage. An hour later she is oozing from cannula sites and has new bruises. Which set of results is most likely?',
 o:['PT ↑, APTT ↑, platelets ↓, fibrinogen ↓, D-dimer ↑','PT normal, APTT ↑, platelets normal, fibrinogen normal, D-dimer normal','PT ↑, APTT normal, platelets normal, fibrinogen normal, D-dimer normal','PT normal, APTT normal, platelets ↓↓, fibrinogen normal, D-dimer normal','PT normal, APTT normal, platelets normal, fibrinogen ↑, D-dimer ↑'],a:0,
 e:'Disseminated intravascular coagulation: placental tissue factor floods the circulation, triggering widespread clotting that consumes platelets, fibrinogen and clotting factors (so PT and APTT lengthen), while fibrinolysis raises D-dimer. The result is clotting and bleeding at once. The other patterns fit haemophilia, early warfarin, ITP, and a simple acute phase response.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 28-year-old man arrives 1 hour after a motorbike crash with a pelvic fracture and haemorrhagic shock. He is given tranexamic acid. How does it reduce bleeding?',
 o:['It blocks the lysine-binding sites on plasminogen so it cannot bind fibrin and be activated','It inhibits tissue plasminogen activator synthesis in the endothelium','It activates factor XIII to cross-link fibrin faster','It replaces consumed fibrinogen','It inhibits antithrombin'],a:0,
 e:'Tranexamic acid is a lysine analogue. Plasminogen and plasmin bind fibrin through lysine-binding sites; blocking them stops clot breakdown, so clots already formed persist. It works best given early (within 3 hours of injury). It does not supply fibrinogen (cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate do) and has no effect on antithrombin or factor XIII.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A healthy woman\'s father has haemophilia A. Her partner does not. What is the chance that any son of theirs will have haemophilia A?',
 o:['0%','25%','50%','100%','12.5%'],a:2,
 e:'Haemophilia A is X-linked recessive. Her father passed his only X chromosome (carrying the faulty F8 gene) to her, so she is an obligate carrier. Each son receives one of her two X chromosomes: a 50% chance of inheriting the affected one. Each daughter has a 50% chance of being a carrier. (The chance that any given child is an affected boy is 25%.)'},
/* ---------- wound healing ---------- */
{t:'wound',type:'sba',q:'A 64-year-old man with obesity has a midline laparotomy. On day 7 after a bout of coughing, serosanguinous fluid leaks from the wound and the edges separate. Why is the wound most vulnerable at this stage?',
 o:['Collagen deposition has only just begun and is mostly weak type III, so tensile strength is low','Neutrophils are digesting the wound edges','The epithelium has not yet bridged the wound','Remodelling has just replaced type I with type III collagen','Platelets have stopped releasing growth factors'],a:0,
 e:'Wound dehiscence typically occurs 5–10 days after surgery, early in the proliferative phase when fibroblasts are laying down a loose, mostly type III collagen matrix. Strength is roughly 10–20% of normal at 1–3 weeks and peaks at about 70–80% after months of remodelling. Epithelium bridges a sutured wound within 24–48 hours. Remodelling replaces type III with type I, not the reverse. Obesity, raised abdominal pressure, infection and malnutrition add risk.'},
{t:'wound',type:'saq',q:'A man on long-term high-dose prednisolone has a leg wound that is healing very slowly. Which vitamin can partly reverse the effect of corticosteroids on wound healing?',acc:['vitamina|retino'],no:['vitaminc','vitamink','vitamind','vitamine'],m:'Vitamin A',
 e:'Corticosteroids blunt the inflammatory phase (fewer macrophages and less growth factor release) and suppress fibroblasts. Vitamin A restores some of the inflammatory response and promotes collagen synthesis, partly counteracting steroids. Vitamin C is needed for collagen hydroxylation generally, but it does not specifically reverse steroid effects.'},
{t:'wound',type:'sba',q:'A 78-year-old man who lives alone and eats mainly tea and toast has swollen bleeding gums, bleeding around hair follicles, corkscrew hairs and a leg wound that will not heal. Which step of collagen production is failing?',
 o:['Hydroxylation of proline and lysine in procollagen','Cross-linking of collagen fibres by lysyl oxidase','Secretion of procollagen by fibroblasts','Breakdown of collagen by matrix metalloproteinases','Transcription of collagen genes'],a:0,
 e:'This is scurvy. Vitamin C (ascorbate) is a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases. Without hydroxylation, collagen triple helices are unstable, so vessels are fragile (perifollicular haemorrhage, gum bleeding) and wounds have no strength. Lysyl oxidase needs copper, not vitamin C. MMPs need zinc.'},
{t:'wound',type:'saq',q:'Which cell, containing α-smooth muscle actin, pulls the edges of an open wound together during healing by secondary intention?',acc:['myofibroblast'],m:'Myofibroblast',
 e:'Under TGF-β and mechanical tension, fibroblasts become myofibroblasts with contractile α-SMA, contracting the wound from about day 5–7. They disappear by apoptosis during remodelling; persistent myofibroblasts cause contractures, especially after burns.'},
{t:'wound',type:'saq',q:'A 22-year-old woman has a firm, shiny, rubbery lump on her earlobe that appeared 6 months after piercing. It extends well beyond the original puncture site and has recurred after a previous excision. What is it?',acc:['keloid'],m:'Keloid scar',
 e:'Keloids grow beyond the boundary of the original wound, rarely regress, often recur after excision, and are commoner in people with darker skin and on the earlobes, chest and shoulders. Hypertrophic scars stay within the wound edges and often improve over 1–2 years. Both involve excess collagen, driven partly by TGF-β; treatment includes intralesional steroid, silicone and pressure.'},
{t:'wound',type:'sba',q:'A 40-year-old man has a 3 cm dog bite laceration on his forearm seen 10 hours after injury. After thorough irrigation and debridement, what is the best approach to closure?',
 o:['Close immediately with tight sutures to eliminate dead space','Leave it open and consider delayed primary closure after a few days if there is no infection','Close with tissue adhesive glue','Excise the wound and apply a skin graft','Close immediately and give no antibiotics'],a:1,
 e:'Bites are heavily contaminated with oral flora (Pasteurella, anaerobes, streptococci). Closing a contaminated wound traps bacteria and risks abscess, so most limb bites are left open and closed later (tertiary or delayed primary intention) once infection is unlikely; facial bites are often closed because of their good blood supply and cosmetic importance. Prophylactic co-amoxiclav is usually given, and tetanus status checked.'},
/* ---------- inflammation ---------- */
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'Within 30 seconds of a firm scratch on the forearm, a red line appears, then a spreading flare and a raised wheal along the scratch. Which mediator, from which cell, is mainly responsible?',
 o:['Histamine from mast cells','Bradykinin from plasma','Prostaglandin E₂ from endothelium','Leukotriene B₄ from neutrophils','C5a from the complement system'],a:0,
 e:'This is Lewis\'s triple response. Preformed histamine is released within seconds from mast cell granules: it dilates arterioles (red line), causes an axon reflex flare, and opens gaps in post-capillary venules (wheal). Prostaglandins and leukotrienes must be newly synthesised and act over minutes; bradykinin and complement come from plasma cascades.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 59-year-old man started ramipril 3 weeks ago. He develops marked swelling of the lips and tongue without itching or urticaria. What is the mechanism?',
 o:['Reduced breakdown of bradykinin','IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation','Inherited C1-inhibitor deficiency','Direct histamine release by the drug','A delayed T cell reaction to the drug'],a:0,
 e:'Angiotensin-converting enzyme is also kininase II, which breaks down bradykinin. ACE inhibitors let bradykinin accumulate, increasing vascular permeability: angioedema without urticaria, which responds poorly to antihistamines. The same mechanism causes the dry cough. Hereditary angioedema is also bradykinin-mediated, but is inherited and would usually have shown itself before.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 41-year-old woman with asthma and nasal polyps takes ibuprofen for a headache and develops severe wheeze within an hour. What is the most likely mechanism?',
 o:['COX blockade diverts arachidonic acid to 5-lipoxygenase, increasing cysteinyl leukotrienes','IgE antibodies against ibuprofen trigger mast cells','Ibuprofen increases COX-2 activity in the airway','Ibuprofen directly releases histamine from basophils','Ibuprofen activates the classical complement pathway'],a:0,
 e:'Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (Samter\'s triad: asthma, nasal polyps, NSAID sensitivity). Blocking COX-1 reduces protective PGE₂ and shunts arachidonic acid down the 5-LOX pathway, producing bronchoconstricting LTC₄, LTD₄ and LTE₄. It is not IgE-mediated and cross-reacts with all COX-1 inhibitors. Leukotriene receptor antagonists such as montelukast can help.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 67-year-old with pneumonia has a pleural effusion. Fluid protein 44 g/L (serum 66 g/L), LDH high, many neutrophils. Which mechanism mainly explains the fluid?',
 o:['Increased vascular permeability from inflammation','Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure','Reduced plasma oncotic pressure','Blocked lymphatic drainage alone','Increased interstitial oncotic pressure from albumin infusion'],a:0,
 e:'Protein-rich, cell-rich fluid (fluid:serum protein ratio above 0.5, high LDH) is an exudate. Inflammatory mediators open gaps between endothelial cells so protein and cells leak out. Transudates (heart failure: raised hydrostatic pressure; nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis: low oncotic pressure) are protein-poor because the endothelial barrier is intact.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 32-year-old woman has bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum and ankle swelling. A node biopsy shows tightly packed non-caseating granulomas. The large pale cells forming the granulomas are derived from which cells?',
 o:['Macrophages activated by IFN-γ','Neutrophils','Plasma cells','Fibroblasts','Mast cells'],a:0,
 e:'This is sarcoidosis. Granulomas are collections of epithelioid cells, which are macrophages activated by IFN-γ from Th1 cells; some fuse into multinucleated giant cells, with a rim of lymphocytes and fibroblasts. Non-caseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis or Crohn\'s disease; caseating ones suggest TB.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'saq',q:'Corticosteroids reduce the production of both prostaglandins and leukotrienes. Which enzyme, at the top of this pathway, do they inhibit indirectly?',acc:['phospholipase|pla2'],m:'Phospholipase A₂',
 e:'Steroids induce annexin-1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A₂, so less arachidonic acid is released from membrane phospholipids. Both the COX (prostaglandins, thromboxane) and 5-LOX (leukotrienes) branches are reduced. NSAIDs act lower down, on COX only.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 2-week-old baby\'s umbilical cord has not separated. The cord stump is red and swollen but there is no pus. The neutrophil count is 35 ×10⁹/L. Which step of neutrophil function is most likely to be defective?',
 o:['Firm adhesion of β2 integrins (LFA-1) to ICAM-1','Rolling on selectins','The respiratory burst','Phagosome–lysosome fusion','Chemotaxis towards C5a'],a:0,
 e:'Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1 (CD18 deficiency): without β2 integrins, neutrophils roll but cannot stick firmly and leave the blood, so blood counts are very high, infected tissue has no pus and the cord separates late. The respiratory burst is missing in chronic granulomatous disease, and phagolysosome fusion fails in Chédiak–Higashi syndrome.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 63-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis receives tocilizumab every 4 weeks. She is admitted with fever, rigors and right lower lobe consolidation, but her CRP is only 6 mg/L. What is the best explanation?',
 o:['Tocilizumab blocks the IL-6 receptor, so the liver does not produce CRP','Bacterial pneumonia does not raise CRP','Tocilizumab causes neutropenia, which lowers CRP','CRP is made by neutrophils, which are suppressed','The bacteria are degrading CRP'],a:0,
 e:'IL-6 is the main stimulus for hepatic acute phase proteins, including CRP. Blocking its receptor suppresses CRP even in serious infection, so a normal CRP is falsely reassuring. Clinicians must rely on clinical signs, cultures and imaging. CRP is made by hepatocytes, not neutrophils.'},
/* ---------- innate ---------- */
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'An 8-year-old boy has had two liver abscesses grown with Staphylococcus aureus and a recent Aspergillus pneumonia. A dihydrorhodamine flow test shows no oxidative burst. Which enzyme is deficient?',
 o:['NADPH oxidase','Myeloperoxidase','Lysozyme','Superoxide dismutase','Catalase'],a:0,
 e:'Chronic granulomatous disease: neutrophils cannot make superoxide via NADPH oxidase, so the respiratory burst fails. Catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus, Serratia, Burkholderia, Nocardia) also destroy the small amount of H₂O₂ they make themselves, leaving the phagocyte nothing to work with. Granulomas form because microbes persist. Myeloperoxidase deficiency is common and usually mild.'},
{t:'innate',type:'saq',q:'Which Toll-like receptor on macrophages recognises the lipid A part of lipopolysaccharide from Gram-negative bacteria?',acc:['tlr4|tolllikereceptor4'],m:'TLR4 (with CD14 and MD-2)',
 e:'TLR4 senses LPS, signalling through MyD88 (and TRIF) to activate NF-κB and release TNF-α, IL-1 and IL-6. In Gram-negative sepsis, massive TLR4 signalling drives vasodilation, capillary leak and DIC. TLR2 senses lipoteichoic acid and lipoproteins; TLR5 flagellin; TLR3 double-stranded RNA; TLR9 CpG DNA.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'A tumour has lost expression of β₂-microglobulin, so its cells no longer display MHC class I. Which cell is best placed to kill it?',
 o:['Natural killer cell','CD8 cytotoxic T cell','CD4 helper T cell','B cell','Eosinophil'],a:0,
 e:'Without β₂-microglobulin, MHC I cannot reach the surface, so CD8 T cells cannot see tumour peptides. NK cells are held back by inhibitory receptors (KIRs, NKG2A) that recognise MHC I; when MHC I is missing ("missing self") and stress ligands are present, they kill with perforin and granzymes. This is a common tumour and herpesvirus escape strategy.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'An 82-year-old woman takes omeprazole long term. Loss of which innate barrier best explains her increased risk of C. difficile infection and bacterial gastroenteritis?',
 o:['Gastric acid','The mucociliary escalator','Lysozyme in tears','Skin keratin','Secretory IgA'],a:0,
 e:'A pH of 1–3 kills most swallowed organisms. Proton pump inhibitors raise gastric pH, so more vegetative bacteria survive to the bowel (Salmonella, Campylobacter, C. difficile). Secretory IgA is adaptive, not an innate barrier, and is not affected by PPIs.'},
{t:'innate',type:'saq',q:'Which family of cytokines, released by virus-infected cells, puts neighbouring cells into an "antiviral state" and increases their MHC class I expression?',acc:['interferon|ifn'],no:['gamma','ifng'],m:'Type I interferons (IFN-α and IFN-β)',
 e:'Viral RNA and DNA sensed by TLR3/7/9, RIG-I and cGAS–STING trigger type I interferon release. Interferons bind IFNAR on nearby cells and induce enzymes such as protein kinase R and 2′,5′-oligoadenylate synthetase that shut down viral protein synthesis and degrade RNA; they also activate NK cells. IFN-γ is type II, made by Th1 and NK cells, and mainly activates macrophages.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'A 45-year-old man collapses with hypotension and widespread urticaria 5 minutes after IV co-amoxiclav during anaesthesia. Which blood test, taken about 1–2 hours after the start of the reaction, best confirms mast cell degranulation?',
 o:['Serum mast cell tryptase','Plasma histamine','Total serum IgE','C-reactive protein','Complement C4'],a:0,
 e:'Tryptase is a mast cell protease that peaks 1–2 hours after degranulation and falls back over 24 hours; RCUK guidance is to take timed samples (as soon as possible after resuscitation, at 1–2 hours, and a baseline at least 24 hours later). Histamine is cleared within minutes, so it is impractical to measure. Total IgE does not confirm an acute reaction.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'A 52-year-old woman on chemotherapy for breast cancer has a temperature of 38.4 °C, feels unwell and has a neutrophil count of 0.3 ×10⁹/L. Her observations are otherwise stable. What should happen first?',
 o:['Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (e.g. piperacillin–tazobactam) within 1 hour, after blood cultures','Wait for blood culture results before treating','Give G-CSF alone','Start oral amoxicillin and review in 48 hours','Give paracetamol and review tomorrow'],a:0,
 e:'Neutropenic sepsis is an emergency. Neutrophils are the main defence against bacteria and fungi, and without them the usual signs (pus, localising signs, sometimes even fever) are blunted, so infection can be overwhelming before it is obvious. NICE recommends empirical IV piperacillin–tazobactam within 1 hour. G-CSF may be added but does not replace antibiotics.'},
/* ---------- complement ---------- */
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'A 19-year-old has recurrent episodes of non-itchy swelling of her lips and hands and attacks of severe abdominal pain. There is never urticaria, and antihistamines and adrenaline do not help. Her father has similar episodes. Which is the best initial screening test?',
 o:['Serum C4','Serum mast cell tryptase','Total IgE','Serum C3 alone','Anti-nuclear antibody'],a:0,
 e:'Hereditary angioedema (autosomal dominant C1-inhibitor deficiency). Without C1-INH, C1 and the kallikrein system are poorly controlled: C4 is continuously consumed (low even between attacks) and bradykinin causes the swelling. C3 is usually normal. Diagnosis is confirmed with C1-INH level and function. Treatment: C1-INH concentrate or icatibant (a bradykinin B2 receptor blocker).'},
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'A 26-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has worsening proteinuria, haematuria and a rising creatinine. Which complement pattern best fits active lupus nephritis?',
 o:['Low C3 and low C4','Low C3 and normal C4','Normal C3 and normal C4','High C3 and high C4','Normal C3 and low C4 only'],a:0,
 e:'Immune complexes deposited in glomeruli activate the classical pathway, consuming both C4 and C3. Falling levels track disease activity. Low C3 with normal C4 suggests alternative pathway consumption (post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, C3 glomerulopathy). Low C4 alone suggests hereditary angioedema.'},
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'A 35-year-old man has dark urine first thing in the morning, haemolytic anaemia and now a hepatic vein thrombosis. Flow cytometry shows that a population of his red cells lacks CD55 and CD59. What is the mechanism of his haemolysis?',
 o:['Uncontrolled formation of the membrane attack complex on red cells','Warm IgG autoantibodies coating red cells','A defect in the red cell cytoskeleton','Oxidative damage from G6PD deficiency','Immune complexes deposited on red cells'],a:0,
 e:'Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria: an acquired PIGA mutation in a stem cell stops GPI anchors being made, so red cells lose DAF (CD55) and CD59 (protectin). Complement then forms MACs on them, causing intravascular haemolysis, haemoglobinuria and thrombosis. It is Coombs negative. Eculizumab or ravulizumab (anti-C5) block the terminal pathway.'},
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'A patient is about to start eculizumab. Which vaccination is essential beforehand?',
 o:['Meningococcal vaccines (MenACWY and MenB)','BCG','Yellow fever','HPV','Rotavirus'],a:0,
 e:'Eculizumab blocks C5 cleavage, so no membrane attack complex can form. MAC-mediated lysis is the key defence against Neisseria, so the risk of meningococcal disease rises roughly a thousand-fold. Patients are vaccinated at least two weeks before starting (or given antibiotic cover) and carry a patient alert card.'},
{t:'compl',type:'saq',q:'Name the complement fragment that is the most potent anaphylatoxin and the main complement chemoattractant for neutrophils.',acc:['c5a'],m:'C5a',
 e:'C5a binds C5aR on neutrophils, monocytes and mast cells: it causes chemotaxis, upregulates integrins and triggers mast cell degranulation. C3a is a weaker anaphylatoxin and is not chemotactic. C3b is the main opsonin; C5b starts the membrane attack complex.'},
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'In the alternative pathway, C3 is spontaneously hydrolysed all the time and C3b lands on any nearby surface. Why are our own cells not destroyed?',
 o:['Factor H, factor I, DAF (CD55) and MCP (CD46) rapidly inactivate C3b on host cells','Host cells have no surface molecules C3b can bind','C3b can only bind lipopolysaccharide','Properdin is absent from human plasma','Antibodies are needed before C3b can deposit'],a:0,
 e:'C3b binds covalently to almost any surface. Host cells carry regulators: factor H (recruited by sialic acid), factor I (cleaves C3b to iC3b), DAF and MCP (dismantle convertases) and CD59 (blocks the MAC). Microbial surfaces lack these, so the C3bBb convertase is stabilised by properdin and amplifies. Factor H mutations cause atypical HUS.'},
/* ---------- lymphatics ---------- */
{t:'lymph',type:'sba',q:'Two years after a mastectomy with axillary node clearance, a 58-year-old woman has a swollen right arm. The swelling was soft at first but has become firm, with thickened skin that no longer pits. Why does chronic lymphoedema become firm?',
 o:['Protein-rich fluid accumulates and drives inflammation, fat deposition and fibrosis','Venous thrombosis has blocked the axillary vein','Plasma albumin has fallen','Heart failure has raised capillary hydrostatic pressure','Histamine has increased capillary permeability'],a:0,
 e:'Lymphatics are the only route for filtered protein to return to the blood. When they are blocked, protein stays in the interstitium, holding water there and stimulating chronic inflammation, adipose deposition and fibrosis, so the limb becomes firm and non-pitting. It also impairs local immunity, so cellulitis is common. Management: compression, skin care, massage and prompt treatment of infection.'},
{t:'lymph',type:'sba',q:'Two days after an oesophagectomy, the chest drain fills with milky white fluid that increases after the patient starts eating. Which structure has most likely been damaged?',
 o:['The thoracic duct','The right lymphatic duct','The azygos vein','The cisterna chyli','The subclavian vein'],a:0,
 e:'This is a chylothorax. Fat absorbed by intestinal lacteals travels as chylomicrons in chyle to the cisterna chyli (in the abdomen at L1–L2), then up the thoracic duct through the posterior mediastinum beside the oesophagus to the left venous angle. Feeding increases chyle flow. The cisterna chyli lies below the diaphragm, out of the operating field.'},
{t:'lymph',type:'sba',q:'A 30-year-old man had a splenectomy after a car crash 5 years ago and has not taken his antibiotics. He develops fever and rapidly progressive sepsis with purpura. Which organism is most likely?',
 o:['Streptococcus pneumoniae','Staphylococcus aureus','Pseudomonas aeruginosa','Candida albicans','Clostridioides difficile'],a:0,
 e:'Overwhelming post-splenectomy infection is classically due to encapsulated bacteria: pneumococcus (most common), meningococcus and Haemophilus influenzae type b. The spleen\'s marginal zone B cells make rapid IgM to polysaccharide capsules, and splenic macrophages clear opsonised bacteria from the blood. Prevention: vaccination, antibiotic prophylaxis and urgent treatment of fever.'},
{t:'lymph',type:'saq',q:'Naive lymphocytes leave the blood to enter the lymph node paracortex through specialised post-capillary vessels. What are these vessels called?',acc:['highendothelial|hev'],m:'High endothelial venules (HEVs)',
 e:'HEVs have tall cuboidal endothelium expressing ligands for L-selectin (CD62L) and chemokines (CCL19, CCL21) that bind CCR7 on naive T and B cells. Lymphocytes leave the node through the efferent lymphatic following sphingosine-1-phosphate; fingolimod blocks this exit.'},
/* ---------- adaptive ---------- */
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'A newborn has seizures from hypocalcaemia, a conotruncal heart defect and, over the next months, recurrent viral and fungal infections. A chest X-ray shows no thymic shadow. Which cells are deficient?',
 o:['Mature T cells','B cells','NK cells','Neutrophils','Complement proteins'],a:0,
 e:'DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion): failed development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches leaves the thymus and parathyroids absent or small. No thymus means few mature T cells (viral and fungal infections); no parathyroids means hypocalcaemia. Remember CATCH-22: Cardiac, Abnormal face, Thymic hypoplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcaemia.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'saq',q:'In antigen-presenting cells, newly made MHC class II molecules carry a protein that blocks the peptide groove so endoplasmic reticulum peptides cannot bind. Name it.',acc:['invariant|cd74'],m:'The invariant chain (CD74), later trimmed to CLIP',
 e:'The invariant chain also directs MHC II to endosomes. There it is degraded to CLIP, which HLA-DM exchanges for an antigenic peptide from engulfed material. This keeps MHC II for exogenous antigen and MHC I (loaded in the ER via TAP) for endogenous antigen.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'A naive CD4 T cell recognises its peptide on MHC II presented by a cell that does not express CD80 or CD86. What is the most likely outcome?',
 o:['The T cell becomes anergic','The T cell expands clonally and becomes Th1','The antigen-presenting cell is killed','The T cell class switches','The T cell becomes a memory cell'],a:0,
 e:'Signal 1 (TCR binding peptide–MHC) without signal 2 (CD28 binding CD80/86) makes the T cell unresponsive (anergy). This is a mechanism of peripheral tolerance: self antigens on non-inflamed tissue are presented without co-stimulation. Infection makes dendritic cells express CD80/86, which is why vaccines need adjuvants. T cells do not class switch; B cells do.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'A kidney transplant recipient takes tacrolimus. Which step in T cell activation does it block?',
 o:['Calcineurin dephosphorylating NFAT, so less IL-2 is transcribed','CD28 binding to CD80/86','IL-2 binding to its receptor','Purine synthesis in dividing lymphocytes','B cell depletion through CD20'],a:0,
 e:'Tacrolimus (with FKBP12) and ciclosporin (with cyclophilin) inhibit calcineurin, so NFAT cannot enter the nucleus and IL-2 is not made. Abatacept blocks CD28–CD80/86; basiliximab blocks the IL-2 receptor (CD25); mycophenolate and azathioprine block purine synthesis; rituximab depletes CD20+ B cells. Calcineurin inhibitors are nephrotoxic and diabetogenic.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'A respiratory virus infects airway epithelial cells but not dendritic cells. How are naive CD8 T cells against this virus primed in the lymph node?',
 o:['Dendritic cells engulf infected cell debris and present the viral peptides on MHC I (cross-presentation)','Dendritic cells present viral peptides on MHC II directly to CD8 T cells','Infected epithelial cells migrate to the lymph node','B cells present viral peptides on MHC I','NK cells transfer the peptides to CD8 T cells'],a:0,
 e:'Cross-presentation, mainly by a subset of dendritic cells, diverts exogenous (engulfed) antigen onto MHC class I so that CD8 T cells can be primed against viruses and tumours that do not infect dendritic cells themselves. CD8 T cells recognise only MHC I, never MHC II.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'saq',q:'Name the transcription factor that defines regulatory T cells, whose loss causes IPEX syndrome.',acc:['foxp3'],m:'FOXP3',
 e:'Tregs (CD4+, CD25+, FOXP3+) suppress other T cells through IL-10, TGF-β, IL-2 consumption and CTLA-4. IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linked) causes severe early-onset autoimmunity: diabetes in infancy, enteropathy and eczema.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'A man with severe psoriasis is treated with an anti-IL-17A monoclonal antibody (secukinumab). Which infection is he most at risk of?',
 o:['Mucocutaneous candidiasis','Pneumocystis pneumonia','Meningococcal disease','Malaria','Helminth infection'],a:0,
 e:'Th17 cells release IL-17, which recruits neutrophils and stimulates antimicrobial peptides at skin and mucosa: the key defence against Candida (and extracellular bacteria). Blocking it, or inherited defects in the IL-17 pathway (e.g. STAT3 in hyper-IgE syndrome), causes chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis. Pneumocystis reflects CD4 T cell loss; meningococcus reflects terminal complement deficiency.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'A 12-year-old has chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism and adrenal insufficiency. A defect in which gene is most likely?',
 o:['AIRE','FOXP3','RAG1','BTK','CD40 ligand'],a:0,
 e:'Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1. AIRE lets medullary thymic epithelial cells express proteins normally found only in other organs (such as the parathyroid and adrenal), so T cells that react to them can be deleted (negative selection, central tolerance). Without AIRE, self-reactive T cells escape. The candidiasis is due to autoantibodies against IL-17 and IL-22.'},
/* ---------- antibodies ---------- */
{t:'idef',type:'saq',q:'A 9-month-old boy has had three episodes of pneumococcal pneumonia and otitis media. His tonsils are barely visible and CD19+ B cells are almost absent. A mutation in which gene is most likely?',acc:['btk|bruton'],m:'BTK (Bruton\'s tyrosine kinase): X-linked agammaglobulinaemia',
 e:'BTK is needed for pre-B cell receptor signalling, so B cell development stops at the pre-B stage: almost no mature B cells, antibodies or tonsillar and nodal follicles. Infections begin after about 6 months as maternal IgG fades, mainly with encapsulated bacteria and enteroviruses. Treatment is lifelong immunoglobulin replacement.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'A rhesus D-negative woman, not given anti-D in her first pregnancy, is now carrying a rhesus D-positive fetus that develops severe anaemia and hydrops. Which antibody is responsible?',
 o:['IgG anti-D','IgM anti-D','IgA anti-D','IgE anti-D','IgD anti-D'],a:0,
 e:'Haemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn is type II hypersensitivity. Sensitisation at the first delivery leads to class-switched, high-affinity IgG anti-D in the next pregnancy, which crosses the placenta via FcRn and coats fetal red cells for destruction in the fetal spleen. IgM (as in ABO antibodies) is a pentamer and cannot cross. Anti-D immunoglobulin given after sensitising events clears fetal cells before the mother responds.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'A woman at 12 weeks of pregnancy develops a rash. Rubella IgM is positive, IgG is positive and the IgG avidity is low. What does this mean?',
 o:['A recent primary rubella infection','Long-standing immunity from childhood vaccination','A false positive result is certain','Reinfection years after vaccination','Passive antibody from a blood transfusion'],a:0,
 e:'IgG avidity rises over months as germinal centre affinity maturation selects better-binding B cells. Low avidity IgG with IgM means the response is only weeks old, indicating a recent primary infection, which in the first trimester carries a high risk of congenital rubella syndrome. High avidity would suggest past infection or reinfection, where the risk is much lower.'},
{t:'idef',type:'sba',q:'A 30-year-old woman with frequent sinus and chest infections has anaphylaxis during her first ever blood transfusion. What underlying condition is most likely?',
 o:['Selective IgA deficiency with anti-IgA antibodies','C1-inhibitor deficiency','X-linked agammaglobulinaemia','IgE deficiency','Chronic granulomatous disease'],a:0,
 e:'Selective IgA deficiency is the commonest primary immunodeficiency (about 1 in 500 white Europeans), often asymptomatic or with mild mucosal infections. Some patients make antibodies against IgA, so IgA in donor plasma can trigger anaphylaxis; they need IgA-deficient or washed blood products. It also causes falsely negative IgA-based coeliac serology.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'A 3-year-old boy has normal to high IgM but very low IgG, IgA and IgE. He has had Pneumocystis pneumonia and Cryptosporidium diarrhoea. Which molecule is most likely defective?',
 o:['CD40 ligand on T cells','Activation-induced cytidine deaminase in B cells','Bruton\'s tyrosine kinase','Complement C3','MHC class I'],a:0,
 e:'X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome: without CD40L, T cells cannot signal B cells to class switch, so IgM is all that is made. CD40L also activates macrophages and dendritic cells, so these patients behave as if they have a T cell defect (Pneumocystis, Cryptosporidium). AID deficiency also causes hyper-IgM, but as a pure B cell defect without opportunistic infections.'},
{t:'ab',type:'saq',q:'Which receptor carries maternal IgG across the placenta and also recycles IgG to give it a long half-life?',acc:['fcrn|neonatalfc'],m:'The neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn)',
 e:'FcRn binds IgG at acidic pH in endosomes and releases it at neutral pH, so IgG is rescued from lysosomal breakdown (half-life about 3 weeks) and transported across the placenta. Monoclonal antibody drugs exploit this; FcRn blockers (efgartigimod) lower pathogenic IgG in myasthenia gravis.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'A 25-year-old gardener with a deep, soil-contaminated wound has had no tetanus vaccine since childhood. He is given human tetanus immunoglobulin as well as a vaccine booster. How does the immunoglobulin mainly protect him?',
 o:['Neutralisation of the toxin','Opsonisation of Clostridium tetani','Complement-mediated lysis of the bacteria','Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity','Activation of mast cells'],a:0,
 e:'Tetanospasmin is an exotoxin; antibody binding blocks it from binding nerve terminals. Neutralisation needs only the Fab arms. Passive immunoglobulin acts immediately, while the toxoid booster generates the patient\'s own longer-lasting antibodies over weeks.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'A 34-year-old with Crohn\'s disease is about to start infliximab. Which investigation is essential before the first dose?',
 o:['Screening for latent tuberculosis','HLA-B27 typing','Total IgE','G6PD activity','TPMT activity'],a:0,
 e:'TNF-α is essential for macrophage activation and for keeping TB walled off in granulomas. Anti-TNF drugs can reactivate latent TB, often as disseminated or extrapulmonary disease, so patients are screened (history, IGRA, chest X-ray) and treated first. TPMT is checked before azathioprine; G6PD before primaquine or dapsone. Hepatitis B and HIV screening are also usual.'},
{t:'ab',type:'saq',q:'Which IgG subclass is the most effective activator of the classical complement pathway?',acc:['igg3|g3'],m:'IgG3',
 e:'C1q binding: IgG3 > IgG1 > IgG2, with IgG4 barely activating complement. IgM (a pentamer) is better still. IgG2 is the main response to polysaccharide antigens; IgG4 rises with chronic antigen exposure (and in IgG4-related disease).'},
/* ---------- hypersensitivity ---------- */
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'A 10-year-old has dark "cola-coloured" urine, puffy eyelids and a blood pressure of 140/95, two weeks after a sore throat. Serum C3 is low and C4 is normal. Which type of hypersensitivity is this?',
 o:['Type III','Type II','Type I','Type IV','It is not a hypersensitivity reaction'],a:0,
 e:'Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis: immune complexes containing streptococcal antigens deposit in glomeruli ("lumpy-bumpy" granular deposits, subepithelial humps) and activate complement (mainly the alternative pathway, so C3 falls), recruiting neutrophils. The 1–3 week latency reflects the time to make antibody. Rheumatic fever, after the same organism, is type II (molecular mimicry).'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'A 25-year-old man has haemoptysis and rapidly rising creatinine. Kidney biopsy shows linear IgG deposits along the glomerular basement membrane. What is the antibody\'s target?',
 o:['The α3 chain of type IV collagen','The phospholipase A₂ receptor','Double-stranded DNA','Proteinase-3','Streptococcal M protein'],a:0,
 e:'Anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture\'s syndrome) is type II hypersensitivity against the NC1 domain of the α3 chain of type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, so it hits the kidney and lungs. Linear staining reflects antibody along a fixed antigen; immune complex disease gives granular staining. PLA₂R antibodies cause membranous nephropathy; PR3-ANCA, granulomatosis with polyangiitis.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'Within ten minutes of starting a blood transfusion, a patient develops fever, loin pain, hypotension and red urine. What is the mechanism?',
 o:['Pre-formed IgM anti-A or anti-B antibodies activate complement, causing intravascular haemolysis','IgE antibodies against plasma proteins trigger mast cells','Donor T cells attack the recipient','Immune complexes deposit in the kidney over days','Donor antibodies against recipient neutrophils damage the lungs'],a:0,
 e:'An ABO-incompatible transfusion is a type II reaction: natural IgM anti-A/anti-B (formed in infancy against gut bacterial antigens) bind donor red cells and activate the classical pathway; the MAC lyses cells in the circulation, releasing haemoglobin and triggering shock and DIC. Stop the transfusion immediately. The last option describes TRALI; the third, transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'Two days after starting to wear a new watch, a woman has an itchy, red, vesicular rash exactly under the metal buckle. Which cells mainly cause this reaction?',
 o:['Memory Th1 cells recruiting and activating macrophages','IgE-armed mast cells','IgG and complement on keratinocytes','Immune complexes in dermal vessels','Neutrophils recruited by IgA'],a:0,
 e:'Allergic contact dermatitis to nickel is type IV (delayed) hypersensitivity. Nickel acts as a hapten, binding skin proteins. On re-exposure, sensitised T cells release IFN-γ and other cytokines and recruit macrophages over 48–72 hours (CD8 T cells also contribute). Patch testing reproduces it. No antibody is involved.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'saq',q:'Ten days after receiving antivenom made in horses, a man develops fever, a rash, joint pains and proteinuria, with low C3. Which Gell and Coombs type of hypersensitivity is this?',acc:['iii|3|immunecomplex'],no:['iv','4'],m:'Type III (serum sickness, immune complex)',
 e:'Serum sickness: the patient makes antibodies to the foreign horse proteins over 7–10 days while plenty of antigen remains, forming soluble immune complexes that deposit in vessels, joints and glomeruli and consume complement. It settles as the antigen is cleared.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'A 30-year-old with anaphylaxis to a wasp sting has received IM adrenaline 500 micrograms twice, 5 minutes apart, plus an IV fluid bolus. He remains hypotensive and wheezy. According to Resuscitation Council UK guidance, what is the next step?',
 o:['Seek expert help and start a low-dose IV adrenaline infusion','Give IV chlorphenamine as the main treatment','Give IV hydrocortisone and wait 30 minutes','Give a third IM dose of 1 mg','Give nebulised salbutamol and observe'],a:0,
 e:'Refractory anaphylaxis is defined as no improvement after two appropriate IM doses of adrenaline. RCUK (2021) recommends getting critical care help early, continuing fluids, and starting an IV adrenaline infusion. Antihistamines only treat skin features and steroids are no longer routine for the acute emergency. The adult IM dose stays at 500 micrograms.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'Minutes after the vascular clamps are released during a kidney transplant, the graft becomes mottled, blue and soft. What is the cause?',
 o:['Pre-formed recipient antibodies against donor ABO or HLA antigens','Donor T cells attacking the recipient','Recipient T cells responding to donor HLA','Chronic vascular intimal thickening','Cytomegalovirus infection of the graft'],a:0,
 e:'Hyperacute rejection: pre-existing antibodies (from previous transplants, pregnancies or transfusions, or ABO mismatch) bind graft endothelium, activate complement and cause immediate thrombosis. It is prevented by ABO matching and a pre-transplant cross-match. Acute cellular rejection (recipient T cells) takes days to weeks; chronic rejection takes months to years.'},
/* ---------- immunodeficiency and HIV ---------- */
{t:'idef',type:'sba',q:'A man with untreated HIV and a CD4 count of 150 cells/mm³ has a 2-week history of dry cough and breathlessness; his oxygen saturation drops from 95% to 86% on walking. Which drug should have been given as prophylaxis?',
 o:['Co-trimoxazole','Fluconazole','Azithromycin','Aciclovir','Isoniazid'],a:0,
 e:'Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia: insidious dry cough, marked exertional desaturation, often a near-normal chest X-ray early on. Prophylaxis with co-trimoxazole is given when CD4 is below 200. Azithromycin prophylaxis is for MAC at very low CD4 counts in some guidelines; isoniazid for latent TB.'},
{t:'idef',type:'sba',q:'A 4-month-old with persistent oral thrush, chronic diarrhoea, failure to thrive and severe lymphopenia is suspected of having severe combined immunodeficiency. Which routine vaccine must not be given?',
 o:['Oral rotavirus vaccine','The 6-in-1 vaccine','Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine','MenB vaccine','Hib-containing vaccines'],a:0,
 e:'Live attenuated vaccines (rotavirus, BCG, MMR, nasal flu, varicella) can cause disseminated infection in SCID. Rotavirus is the live vaccine in the infant schedule at 8 and 12 weeks. Inactivated, subunit and conjugate vaccines are safe but may not produce a response. SCID is now included in newborn screening in England (TREC test).'},
{t:'idef',type:'saq',q:'Early in infection HIV usually uses a chemokine co-receptor alongside CD4. People homozygous for a 32-base-pair deletion in this receptor are largely resistant to infection. Name the co-receptor.',acc:['ccr5'],m:'CCR5',
 e:'HIV gp120 binds CD4, then CCR5 (R5 virus, usually early) or CXCR4 (X4 virus, often late, associated with faster progression). The CCR5-Δ32 mutation protects homozygotes, and stem cell transplants from such donors have cured a few people. Maraviroc blocks CCR5.'},
/* ---------- vaccines ---------- */
{t:'vacc',type:'sba',q:'Babies under 2 respond poorly to pure polysaccharide vaccines, so Hib, pneumococcal and MenACWY vaccines are given as conjugates. Why does conjugation help?',
 o:['Linking the polysaccharide to a carrier protein recruits T cell help, giving class switching, affinity maturation and memory','Conjugation makes the polysaccharide large enough to activate complement','The carrier protein directly activates NK cells','Conjugation lets the vaccine cross the placenta','Conjugation allows the vaccine to be live attenuated'],a:0,
 e:'Polysaccharides are T-independent antigens: they cross-link B cell receptors, giving mostly IgM and little memory, and infants\' marginal zone B cells are immature. In a conjugate, B cells that bind the polysaccharide internalise the attached protein and present its peptides on MHC II to helper T cells, which then provide CD40L and cytokines: IgG, memory and response to boosters.'},
{t:'vacc',type:'saq',q:'A new virus has a basic reproduction number (R₀) of 10. Roughly what percentage of the population must be immune to reach the herd immunity threshold? (Give a number.)',acc:['90'],no:['95','99','19'],m:'About 90% (1 − 1/R₀ = 1 − 1/10)',
 e:'The herd immunity threshold is 1 − 1/R₀. For R₀ = 10 this is 0.9. Measles, with R₀ of 12–18, needs about 95% coverage, which is why falling MMR uptake allows outbreaks. Vaccine effectiveness below 100% pushes the coverage needed higher still.'},
{t:'vacc',type:'sba',q:'Which of these patients should NOT be given the MMR vaccine at this time?',
 o:['A woman who is 16 weeks pregnant','A child with egg allergy','A child with a mild cold and no fever','A breastfeeding mother','An adult with HIV and a stable CD4 count of 450 cells/mm³'],a:0,
 e:'MMR is live attenuated, so it is avoided in pregnancy (theoretical risk to the fetus; women should avoid pregnancy for 1 month after) and in severe immunosuppression. Egg allergy is not a contraindication because the measles and mumps components are grown in chick fibroblast cultures with negligible egg protein. Minor illness without fever, breastfeeding and well-controlled HIV with CD4 above 200 are not contraindications.'},
{t:'vacc',type:'saq',q:'The tetanus and diphtheria vaccines contain inactivated bacterial toxins rather than whole organisms. What is this type of vaccine called?',acc:['toxoid'],m:'Toxoid vaccine',
 e:'Toxoids are exotoxins inactivated with formaldehyde: they keep their antigenic shape but lose toxicity, so the antibodies made neutralise the real toxin. Endotoxin (lipid A) cannot be made into a toxoid.'},
/* ---------- microbes ---------- */
{t:'path',type:'sba',q:'An 18-year-old student has fever, headache, neck stiffness and a rapidly spreading non-blanching purpuric rash. Gram-negative diplococci are seen in the CSF. What mainly causes the rash?',
 o:['Endotoxin-driven DIC and damage to small vessels causing bleeding into the skin','IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation in the skin','A type IV reaction to the bacteria','Immune thrombocytopenia from antiplatelet antibodies','A secreted exotoxin that digests the skin'],a:0,
 e:'Neisseria meningitidis sheds large amounts of lipooligosaccharide endotoxin, triggering cytokine release, endothelial damage and DIC. Bleeding into the skin causes petechiae and purpura that do not blanch under a glass, and in severe cases purpura fulminans. Give IM or IV benzylpenicillin before hospital if meningococcal disease is suspected.'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'A man develops double vision, drooping eyelids, dilated pupils and then a descending flaccid paralysis two days after eating home-preserved vegetables. He is alert with no sensory loss. How does the toxin act?',
 o:['It cleaves SNARE proteins, so acetylcholine cannot be released at the neuromuscular junction','It blocks glycine and GABA release from inhibitory interneurons','It inhibits elongation factor 2 and stops protein synthesis','It raises cAMP in enterocytes','It is a superantigen that activates many T cells'],a:0,
 e:'Botulinum toxin (Clostridium botulinum) is taken up at cholinergic terminals and cleaves SNAREs (SNAP-25, synaptobrevin, syntaxin), preventing vesicle fusion: flaccid paralysis, starting with cranial nerves, plus autonomic features. Tetanus toxin cleaves synaptobrevin in inhibitory interneurons, causing spastic paralysis. EF-2 is targeted by diphtheria toxin; cAMP by cholera toxin.'},
{t:'path',type:'sba',q:'Which Staphylococcus aureus surface protein binds the Fc portion of IgG the "wrong way round", preventing opsonisation?',
 o:['Protein A','Coagulase','Panton–Valentine leukocidin','Toxic shock syndrome toxin-1','M protein'],a:0,
 e:'Protein A binds IgG Fc, so antibody coats the bacterium with its Fab arms facing outward and phagocyte Fc receptors cannot engage. It is used in labs to purify antibodies. Coagulase forms a fibrin coat; PVL lyses neutrophils; TSST-1 is a superantigen; M protein belongs to Streptococcus pyogenes.'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'Eight months after a hip replacement, a 70-year-old has a painful, loosening joint. Staphylococcus epidermidis is grown from several tissue samples. Six weeks of IV vancomycin have not cleared it. What is the main reason?',
 o:['The bacteria live in a biofilm on the prosthesis that shields them from antibiotics and phagocytes','S. epidermidis always produces vancomycin-destroying enzymes','The bacteria form spores inside bone','S. epidermidis survives inside macrophages','The polysaccharide capsule blocks all antibiotics'],a:0,
 e:'Coagulase-negative staphylococci form biofilms on implants: a matrix that slows antibiotic penetration and protects slow-growing, metabolically inactive "persister" cells that β-lactams and glycopeptides cannot kill. Cure usually needs removal or exchange of the device, often with rifampicin-containing combination therapy. S. epidermidis does not form spores.'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'High-risk human papillomavirus types (16 and 18) cause cervical cancer. What is the main mechanism?',
 o:['Viral E6 and E7 proteins inactivate p53 and Rb','The virus integrates using its own reverse transcriptase','Chronic inflammation alone damages the epithelium','The virus inserts next to the MYC gene in every case','The virus produces a superantigen'],a:0,
 e:'E6 targets p53 for degradation and E7 binds and inactivates Rb, removing two key brakes on the cell cycle and DNA damage response. HPV is a DNA virus with no reverse transcriptase. The HPV vaccine (a recombinant virus-like particle vaccine, now a single dose at 12–13 years in the UK) prevents infection with these types.'},
{t:'micro',type:'saq',q:'Mycobacteria have a waxy mycolic acid wall and stain poorly with the Gram stain. Name a stain used to detect them in sputum.',acc:['ziehl|neelsen|auramine|acidfast'],m:'Ziehl–Neelsen (acid-fast) stain, or auramine–rhodamine fluorescence',
 e:'Ziehl–Neelsen uses heated carbol fuchsin, which mycobacteria retain even after acid-alcohol decolourisation ("acid-fast bacilli", pink-red on a blue background). Auramine fluorescent staining is more sensitive for screening. Culture takes weeks, so PCR (e.g. GeneXpert) also gives rapid identification and rifampicin resistance.'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'Why are instruments used in a brain biopsy of a patient with suspected Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease quarantined or destroyed?',
 o:['Misfolded prion protein resists standard autoclaving and disinfectants','Prions are bacteria that form spores','Prions are enveloped viruses that survive on steel','Prions are killed only by alcohol','Prions survive only in the patient\'s blood'],a:0,
 e:'Prions are misfolded host prion protein (PrPSc) that converts normal PrP into the misfolded form. They contain no nucleic acid and resist standard heat sterilisation, irradiation and most disinfectants, so contaminated neurosurgical and ophthalmic instruments can transmit CJD (iatrogenic CJD).'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'A 20-year-old student has a dry cough, headache and low-grade fever for 10 days, with patchy changes on chest X-ray. A course of amoxicillin has not helped. Cold agglutinins are positive. Why did amoxicillin fail?',
 o:['The organism has no peptidoglycan cell wall','The organism lives only inside neutrophils','The organism always produces β-lactamase','The organism forms spores','The organism has an outer membrane impermeable to all drugs'],a:0,
 e:'Mycoplasma pneumoniae has no cell wall, so β-lactams (which block peptidoglycan cross-linking) cannot work. It causes "atypical" pneumonia in young adults, sometimes with cold agglutinin haemolysis and erythema multiforme. Macrolides or doxycycline, which act on the ribosome, are used.'},
{t:'path',type:'sba',q:'A 15-year-old develops migrating painful joints, a new heart murmur and subcutaneous nodules three weeks after an untreated sore throat. What is the mechanism of the heart damage?',
 o:['Antibodies against streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin (molecular mimicry)','Streptococci directly invade the myocardium','Immune complexes deposit in the heart valves','A circulating toxin damages heart muscle','A superantigen activates T cells non-specifically'],a:0,
 e:'Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis. Antibodies (and T cells) against M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin, valve glycoproteins, joints and brain (Sydenham chorea): a type II hypersensitivity by molecular mimicry. Recurrent episodes cause chronic rheumatic valve disease, most often of the mitral valve. Prompt penicillin for strep throat prevents it.'},
{t:'path',type:'sba',q:'A 17-year-old with glandular fever has a blood film showing many large "atypical lymphocytes". What are most of these cells?',
 o:['CD8 T cells responding to EBV-infected B cells','EBV-infected B cells','NK cells','Monocytes','Plasma cells'],a:0,
 e:'Epstein–Barr virus infects B cells through CD21 (complement receptor 2). The atypical lymphocytes are mostly activated CD8 cytotoxic T cells expanding to kill infected B cells; they cause the lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. Heterophile (Paul–Bunnell, Monospot) antibodies support the diagnosis. Amoxicillin often causes a rash.'},
{t:'path',type:'saq',q:'Toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 binds MHC class II and the T cell receptor outside the peptide groove, activating up to 20% of all T cells at once. What is this kind of molecule called?',acc:['superantigen'],m:'A superantigen',
 e:'Superantigens bypass normal antigen specificity by clamping MHC II to particular TCR Vβ regions, causing massive release of IL-2, TNF-α and IFN-γ: fever, rash, hypotension and multi-organ failure. Causes include TSST-1 (S. aureus, classically with tampons or packing) and streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins.'},
/* ---------- diagnosis ---------- */
{t:'diag',type:'sba',q:'A 22-year-old has fever, headache and neck stiffness. CSF: cloudy; white cells 1200/µL, 90% neutrophils; protein 2.1 g/L; CSF:blood glucose ratio 0.2. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
 o:['Bacterial meningitis','Viral meningitis','Tuberculous meningitis','Subarachnoid haemorrhage','Normal CSF'],a:0,
 e:'Bacterial meningitis gives turbid CSF with many neutrophils, high protein and low glucose (bacteria and neutrophils consume it). Viral meningitis: lymphocytes, mildly raised protein, normal glucose. TB meningitis: lymphocytes, very high protein and low glucose, often with a subacute history. Normal CSF glucose is about 60% of blood glucose.'},
{t:'diag',type:'sba',q:'Which is the best practice when taking blood cultures from a patient with suspected sepsis?',
 o:['Take them from a fresh venepuncture before antibiotics, clean the skin with chlorhexidine–alcohol and let it dry, and fill aerobic and anaerobic bottles','Take them from the existing cannula to avoid another needle','Wait until after the first antibiotic dose so the result reflects treatment','Fill only the anaerobic bottle to save blood','Store the bottles in a fridge until the next morning'],a:0,
 e:'Contamination with skin flora (coagulase-negative staphylococci) causes false positives and unnecessary antibiotics, so use aseptic non-touch technique with a fresh venepuncture, and do not draw from old cannulas. Take cultures before antibiotics (antibiotics lower the yield), but never delay antibiotics in sepsis for them. Send promptly at room temperature for incubation.'},
{t:'diag',type:'saq',q:'Antibiotic sensitivity reports often give an MIC for each drug. What does MIC stand for?',acc:['minimuminhibitory|minimalinhibitory'],m:'Minimum inhibitory concentration',
 e:'The MIC is the lowest antibiotic concentration that prevents visible growth in vitro. It is compared with breakpoints to report sensitive, intermediate (susceptible at increased exposure) or resistant. Time-dependent drugs (β-lactams) aim to keep levels above the MIC for most of the dosing interval.'},
/* ---------- antimicrobials ---------- */
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'Why is vancomycin ineffective against Escherichia coli?',
 o:['It is too large to pass through the porins in the Gram-negative outer membrane','E. coli has no peptidoglycan','E. coli β-lactamases destroy vancomycin','E. coli pumps vancomycin out via the same efflux pump as tetracycline','E. coli methylates the 50S ribosome'],a:0,
 e:'Vancomycin, a large glycopeptide, binds the D-Ala–D-Ala end of peptidoglycan precursors. Gram-negatives do have peptidoglycan, but the outer membrane keeps vancomycin out. It is used for Gram-positive organisms, including MRSA, and orally (unabsorbed) for C. difficile. It is not a β-lactam, so β-lactamases do not affect it.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A 77-year-old with pyelonephritis is started on IV gentamicin. He also takes high-dose furosemide for heart failure. Which adverse effect is the main concern with this combination?',
 o:['Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity','QT prolongation','Tendon rupture','Red man syndrome','Serotonin syndrome'],a:0,
 e:'Aminoglycosides accumulate in the inner ear (hair cells) and proximal tubule. Loop diuretics are also ototoxic and dehydration raises gentamicin levels, so the risks add up. Ototoxicity can be permanent (vestibular and cochlear). Gentamicin is given once daily (concentration-dependent killing) with trough level monitoring and dose adjusted for kidney function.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A 69-year-old taking simvastatin 40 mg is prescribed clarithromycin for a chest infection. A week later he has muscle pain, weakness and dark urine. What is the mechanism?',
 o:['Clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4, raising simvastatin levels and causing myopathy and rhabdomyolysis','Clarithromycin itself directly damages muscle','Clarithromycin induces CYP3A4, producing a toxic simvastatin metabolite','Clarithromycin causes immune complex myositis','Clarithromycin blocks the neuromuscular junction'],a:0,
 e:'Clarithromycin and erythromycin are strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (azithromycin much less so). Simvastatin and atorvastatin are metabolised by CYP3A4, so levels rise sharply. Statins should be paused during a clarithromycin course. Macrolides also prolong the QT interval.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A woman who is 8 weeks pregnant has a urinary tract infection. Why should trimethoprim be avoided?',
 o:['It is a folate antagonist, which raises the risk of neural tube defects in the first trimester','It discolours developing teeth','It causes grey baby syndrome','It damages fetal cartilage and tendons','It is ototoxic to the fetus'],a:0,
 e:'Trimethoprim inhibits dihydrofolate reductase. It is far more selective for the bacterial enzyme, but at the time of neural tube closure any folate antagonism is avoided. In the first trimester nitrofurantoin is usually used (avoided at term because of neonatal haemolysis). Tetracyclines stain teeth; chloramphenicol causes grey baby syndrome; quinolones affect cartilage; aminoglycosides are ototoxic.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'Why does aciclovir have very few side effects compared with most anticancer nucleoside analogues?',
 o:['Its first phosphorylation is carried out by viral thymidine kinase, so it is activated mainly in infected cells','It is only absorbed by infected cells','It binds human DNA polymerase more strongly than viral polymerase','It is activated by the host cell\'s own thymidine kinase in all dividing cells','It blocks viral entry rather than replication'],a:0,
 e:'HSV and VZV thymidine kinase phosphorylates aciclovir far more efficiently than host kinases. Host enzymes then make aciclovir triphosphate, which preferentially inhibits viral DNA polymerase and terminates the DNA chain. Uninfected cells barely activate it. Thymidine kinase mutation is the usual resistance mechanism, and foscarnet (needs no activation) is then used.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A 55-year-old on chemotherapy for leukaemia is profoundly neutropenic and has invasive pulmonary aspergillosis. What is the first-line antifungal?',
 o:['Voriconazole','Fluconazole','Nystatin','Terbinafine','Griseofulvin'],a:0,
 e:'Voriconazole (or isavuconazole) is first line for invasive aspergillosis; liposomal amphotericin is an alternative. Fluconazole has no useful activity against moulds such as Aspergillus. Nystatin is not absorbed and is used topically; terbinafine is for dermatophytes; griseofulvin for tinea capitis. Voriconazole causes visual disturbance and many CYP450 interactions.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A 25-year-old woman taking the combined oral contraceptive pill starts treatment for tuberculosis. What advice is most important?',
 o:['Use additional or alternative contraception, because rifampicin induces liver enzymes and reduces hormone levels','There is no interaction; continue as normal','Rifampicin increases oestrogen levels, raising clot risk','Isoniazid inactivates the pill in the gut','Take the pill at a different time of day from her TB drugs'],a:0,
 e:'Rifampicin is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 and other enzymes, lowering levels of oestrogens and progestogens (contraceptive failure), warfarin, many antiretrovirals, steroids and more. A copper IUD, levonorgestrel IUS or depot injection are recommended. Rifampicin also turns tears, urine and contact lenses orange.'},
{t:'drug',type:'saq',q:'Which vitamin is co-prescribed with isoniazid to prevent peripheral neuropathy?',acc:['pyridoxine|b6'],m:'Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)',
 e:'Isoniazid binds pyridoxine and increases its excretion, causing a sensory peripheral neuropathy, especially in people who are malnourished, diabetic, alcohol-dependent, pregnant, have HIV or are slow acetylators. Isoniazid also causes hepatitis; monitor liver function.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'An 80-year-old has an E. coli bloodstream infection. The laboratory reports an extended-spectrum β-lactamase (ESBL)-producing organism, resistant to ceftriaxone. Which antibiotic is most reliable?',
 o:['Meropenem','Amoxicillin','Co-amoxiclav','A higher dose of ceftriaxone','Flucloxacillin'],a:0,
 e:'ESBLs hydrolyse penicillins and third-generation cephalosporins. Carbapenems are stable against them and are the most reliable choice for serious ESBL infections. Clavulanic acid inhibits ESBLs in the test tube, but co-amoxiclav is unreliable in serious infections. Overuse of carbapenems drives carbapenemase-producing Enterobacterales (CPE), so they are reserved.'},
{t:'drug',type:'saq',q:'Name the oral neuraminidase inhibitor used to treat influenza in at-risk patients.',acc:['oseltamivir|tamiflu'],m:'Oseltamivir',
 e:'Neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid so newly budded virions can leave the cell and spread through mucus. Oseltamivir (oral) and zanamivir (inhaled) block it; they work best within 48 hours of symptom onset and are given to at-risk groups (and in care home outbreaks). Baloxavir instead blocks the viral cap-dependent endonuclease.'},
/* ---------- infection control ---------- */
{t:'ipc',type:'sba',q:'A 30-year-old unvaccinated man is admitted with fever, cough, conjunctivitis and a maculopapular rash that started behind his ears; measles is suspected. What precautions are needed?',
 o:['Airborne precautions: a negative-pressure side room and FFP3 respirators for staff','Droplet precautions only, with a fluid-resistant surgical mask','Contact precautions only, with gloves and an apron','Standard precautions only','Cohort him in a bay with other febrile patients'],a:0,
 e:'Measles spreads by the airborne route: tiny droplet nuclei stay suspended and travel between rooms, and it is extremely infectious (R₀ 12–18). Airborne precautions are also needed for pulmonary TB and aerosol-generating procedures. Staff should have documented immunity, and exposed susceptible contacts may need post-exposure MMR or immunoglobulin.'},
{t:'ipc',type:'sba',q:'A foundation doctor sustains a deep needlestick injury from a patient known to have HIV with a detectable viral load. After first aid, what should happen about HIV post-exposure prophylaxis?',
 o:['Start PEP as soon as possible, ideally within hours and no later than 72 hours','Wait for the source patient\'s viral load result before deciding','Start PEP within 7 days if symptoms develop','PEP is not needed after needlestick injuries','Give an HIV vaccine instead'],a:0,
 e:'PEP (a short course of antiretrovirals, usually for 28 days) is most effective the sooner it starts, and is not recommended after 72 hours. Occupational health or the emergency department assesses the risk; hepatitis B immunity is checked and boosted if needed. First aid: encourage bleeding, wash with soap and running water, do not scrub or suck.'},
{t:'ipc',type:'sba',q:'A 26-year-old takes MDMA at a festival and arrives agitated with a temperature of 41.5 °C, hot, dry skin, and a rising CK. Why will paracetamol not lower his temperature?',
 o:['His hypothalamic set point is normal; heat production simply exceeds heat loss','Paracetamol is inactivated by MDMA','His set point has been raised too far for paracetamol to reverse','MDMA blocks COX-2 already','Paracetamol only works in bacterial fever'],a:0,
 e:'This is hyperthermia, not fever. Antipyretics lower a raised set point by reducing hypothalamic PGE₂; in hyperthermia the set point is normal and the body is already trying to cool itself, so treatment is active external cooling (and dantrolene is sometimes considered). Complications include rhabdomyolysis, DIC, kidney injury and hyponatraemia.'}
]};
